# JavaScript and TypeScript Engineering Gates

This rule owns mandatory policy only. Use the [TypeScript and JavaScript skill](file:///Users/Devesh_Padmanabhan/.cursor/agent-engineering-handbook/skills/typescript-javascript/SKILL.md) for language patterns, configuration, testing, performance, and examples.

## Shared Runtime and Supply-Chain Gates

- Pin the supported Node.js runtime and package-manager version. Use the committed lock file with immutable CI installation.
- Do not use `eval`, `Function`, unsafe deserialization, or shell-interpreted command strings with untrusted input.
- Validate external data at runtime before assigning domain types. Type declarations do not validate JSON, environment variables, messages, storage, or request bodies.
- Keep secrets out of source, browser bundles, logs, errors, command arguments, and committed configuration.
- Use ESM unless repository compatibility requires another module format; do not mix module systems accidentally.

## HTTP and Server Gates

- Reuse clients or agents where the runtime supports pooling. Every outbound request requires a timeout or abort signal, status handling, bounded body processing, and runtime response validation.
- Validate URLs and constrain outbound destinations when input can influence a fetch. Do not follow redirects into untrusted networks without revalidation.
- Servers require bounded request bodies, explicit CORS policy, security headers, structured error handling, timeouts, and graceful shutdown.
- Authentication and authorization are separate. Enforce resource authorization on the server for every protected operation.
- Await or deliberately supervise every promise. Fire-and-forget work requires explicit lifecycle ownership and error reporting.

## JavaScript Gates

- Production JavaScript uses `// @ts-check` and JSDoc types unless the repository documents an alternative static-analysis contract.
- Exported JavaScript APIs document parameter, return, callback, generic, and shared object types with precise JSDoc.
- Do not silence type errors with broad `@ts-ignore`; use a narrow `@ts-expect-error` only with a reason and a testable compatibility boundary.
- Treat JSDoc assertions as compile-time aids, never as runtime validation.

## TypeScript Gates

- Enable strict type checking. Do not weaken `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, or related safety settings merely to make code compile.
- Exported functions and public methods have explicit parameter and return types; obvious local variables use inference.
- Avoid `any`; prefer `unknown` and narrow it. Prohibit double assertions and `as any` at trust boundaries.
- Use discriminated unions, exhaustive checks, and precise return types for stateful or failure-prone behavior.
- Do not use non-null assertions unless an invariant is established locally and cannot be expressed more safely.

## Public API Documentation

- Add TSDoc or JSDoc when exported callers need behavior, errors, ownership, blocking, side effects, constraints, or lifecycle details that types cannot express.
- Use tags only when they add contract information. Do not write comments that merely restate identifiers or signatures.

## Verification

- Run the repository's tests, lint with zero warnings, formatter check, type check, and build/package step before completion.
- Run the package manager's dependency audit when dependencies or lock files change.
- Add focused tests for changed success, validation, timeout, cancellation, authorization, and shutdown behavior where applicable.
